### §5.0 — Epistemic Release Protocol

The release process determines whether incoming information is ready to be communicated as knowledge.

| Stage | Action     | Release requirement                       |
| ----- | ---------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| 01    | Receive    | Original information preserved            |
| 02    | Identify   | Source established where possible         |
| 03    | Transcribe | Wording retained                          |
| 04    | Classify   | Epistemic category assigned               |
| 05    | Interpret  | Meaning documented                        |
| 06    | Correlate  | Relationships identified                  |
| 07    | Verify     | Evidence examined                         |
| 08    | Qualify    | Uncertainty and limitations disclosed     |
| 09    | Bind       | Terminology and provenance connected      |
| 10    | Review     | Independent examination where appropriate |
| 11    | Release    | Findings communicated accurately          |
| 12    | Archive    | Documentary integrity maintained          |

### Release classifications

* GREEN — Verified: Adequate evidence supports the stated conclusion.

* CYAN — Corroborated: Independent information provides additional support.

* YELLOW — Qualified: Information is usable with disclosed limitations.

* AMBER — Pending: Verification remains incomplete.

* RED — Disputed: Material evidentiary conflicts remain unresolved.

* GREY — Unverified: Insufficient evidence to establish the claim.

These are proposed workflow labels, not scientific confidence scores.

